course: dictionary
lesson: 14191
title: Strong feeling words
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words show a feeling that is much stronger than you might expect. «Mortified» is one of them. «Mia was mortified when her mum sang loudly at the school gate.» Look at the clues: singing loudly at the gate is embarrassing, and the word shows a bigger feeling than a small blush.
explain: Ask what word would fit in the gap. «Tom tripped on the stage and felt ___.» Embarrassed would fit, and mortified fits too, but it means much more: his face went red and he wanted the floor to swallow him up.
explain: Check your meaning by putting it into the sentence. Mortified means extremely embarrassed or ashamed. «The silly joke mortified Sam in front of his friends» means the joke made Sam feel very embarrassed. It makes sense, so the meaning is right.
fact: Mortified means extremely embarrassed or ashamed.
fact: «Mortified» is a much stronger word than «embarrassed».
fact: To mortify someone is to make them feel very embarrassed: «The silly joke mortified Sam.»
fact: Common phrases are «absolutely mortified», «I was mortified» and «a mortifying moment».
fact: Long ago, in church history, to mortify the body meant to make it suffer on purpose, for example by fasting. This is an old meaning, not an everyday one.
exercise: «Mia was mortified when her mum sang loudly at the school gate.» What does mortified mean? | Very happy and excited | Very hungry | *Extremely embarrassed | Tired and sleepy
exercise: «It was a mortifying moment when his phone rang in the quiet test.» What does mortifying suggest? | *Something very embarrassing | Something very quiet and calm | Something very funny and kind | Something that happened last year
exercise: «Tom tripped on the stage and felt mortified.» Find and copy one word that shows a strong feeling. | stage | tripped | Tom | *mortified
test: Dad danced at the school disco. Ella said, «I am absolutely mortified!» How does Ella feel? | Proud and happy | Hungry | *Very embarrassed | Sleepy
test: Nadia says mortified is just another word for embarrassed. Why might an author choose mortified? | It means exactly the same, so it is never needed | *It shows a much stronger feeling than embarrassed | It means happy and calm | It is only used in church
exam: «After the fire alarm went off during the spelling test, Jack felt mortified.» What is the best meaning of mortified here? | Bored and tired | Very cold | *Extremely embarrassed or ashamed | Pleased and proud
